nodecode-freshdesk is a rewrite of aedelgod's aedelgod/hermes-plugin-freshdesk:

  https://github.com/aedelgod/hermes-plugin-freshdesk
  commit 86d2f5312c1a1184516bef649ecb16f3fccc9011
  MIT License, Copyright (c) GFORGOV® (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's eleven model-facing Freshdesk v2 tools with their
names, argument schemas and answer shapes -- bounded ticket pagination with
the truncated flag, the filtered ticket search that builds and URL-encodes its
own query, one-ticket and one-contact reads, the bounded conversation list,
the attachment download that resolves its URL from the ticket, and the five
writes (private note, create, reply, time entry, update) with their plain-text
HTML escaping, their canonical H:MM durations, and the readback that compares
every field before success. Also kept: the account-origin rules (a native
https://<account>.freshdesk.com, never a custom CNAME), the Basic-auth header
that never appears in a URL or an answer, the attachment-origin tiers and the
dot-segment check that decide whether the key travels with a download, the
4 MiB answer and 25 MiB attachment ceilings, Freshdesk's own words in a
refusal, and the write_may_have_succeeded state with retry_safe false for a
write that could not be verified. The Python (urllib, base64, html.escape,
re) is Common Lisp here: nlk:http for the wire, one section declaration for
the settings, and eleven nlk:define-contract declarations for the tools'
arguments, so the schema the model is shown and the body that runs are one
shape.

Left out: the plugin's pre_tool_call hook, which escalates its five write
tools to Hermes' human-approval gate with a per-argument hashed rule key and
an approval reason naming the pending change. That gate is a Hermes runtime
surface with no cell counterpart, so in this rewrite the writes run when the
model calls them and the readback is the only check. Also replaced rather
than ported: Hermes' profile-scoped secret reader becomes the
api_key_env/api_key_file settings, and the Hermes-home cache becomes the
download_dir setting. Not carried over either: the plugin's own redirect
rejection (the image's HTTP layer follows a redirect, whichever way it goes,
and the attachment origin is checked before the fetch, not after) and its
NOFOLLOW directory walk (the attachment is created exclusively under the
operator's own download directory, mode 0600 where the system keeps modes).
